## Rules and aggregation

Rules are tracked only when they are configured. For a ClusterRole with an
`aggregation_rule`, the Kubernetes controller manager owns `.rules` and rewrites them
from the selected ClusterRoles, so `rule` stays empty in state. `rule` blocks
configured next to an `aggregation_rule` are still sent to the API but are never read
back, so Terraform plans an in-place update for them on every run. Remove them, and
grant permissions to an aggregated role through a ClusterRole that the
`aggregation_rule` selects.

The `rule` attributes `api_groups`, `non_resource_urls`, `resource_names`, `resources`
and `verbs` are sets, so element order is not significant. They must not contain
`null` elements. Omitted rule attributes default to an empty set, and `api_groups`
must contain at least one element when it is set. A rule that targets
`non_resource_urls` omits `api_groups`.

## Import

ClusterRole can be imported using the name, e.g.

```
$ terraform import kubernetes_cluster_role_v1.example terraform-name
```

Identity imports use `api_version = "rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1"`,
`kind = "ClusterRole"` and `name`. An identity with a different `kind` or
`api_version` is rejected.

To import a ClusterRole that was created with `generateName`, configure
`generate_name` with its original prefix and omit `name`. Configuring `name` instead
plans a replacement.

## Metadata and upgrades

Exactly one `metadata` block is required. RBAC names may contain colons, such as
`system:example`; they are not restricted to DNS names. Set either `name` or
`generate_name`, not both. Label and annotation values must be non-null strings;
omit an entry to remove it.

Upgrading SDKv2 state preserves the ClusterRole and its name-only ID. If
configuration explicitly sets `labels = {}` or `annotations = {}`, the first plan may
show a one-time in-place update because SDKv2 stored empty maps as null. The
ClusterRole is not replaced.

For a ClusterRole with an `aggregation_rule`, SDKv2 recorded the rules that the
controller manager copied into state. The Framework resource does not track them, so
upgrading does not plan their removal.

## Migrating from `kubernetes_cluster_role`

With Terraform 1.8 or later, rename the deprecated `kubernetes_cluster_role` resource
type to `kubernetes_cluster_role_v1`, retain its configuration, and add:

```terraform
moved {
  from = kubernetes_cluster_role.example
  to   = kubernetes_cluster_role_v1.example
}
```

Terraform records the address change without replacing the ClusterRole. A move-only
plan may return exit code 2 with `-detailed-exitcode`, even with no infrastructure
changes. After applying the move, an unchanged configuration plans no changes.
Existing `kubernetes_cluster_role_v1` configurations do not need a `moved` block when
upgrading the provider, including upgrades from provider 2.37.1 before resource
identity was introduced.
